      <!-- FAQ: Difference between TDEE and maintenance calories -->
      <details class="group bg-canvas-elevated border border-hairline rounded-xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between font-semibold text-ink text-sm sm:text-base">
          <span>What is the difference between TDEE and maintenance calories?</span>
          <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
            <svg fill="none" height="20" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"></path></svg>
          </span>
        </summary>
        <p class="text-body mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">
          TDEE (Total Daily Energy Expenditure) and maintenance calories refer to the exact same caloric figure. TDEE is the physiological term describing total daily energy burn, while maintenance calories is the dietary term describing the daily caloric intake needed to match that expenditure and keep your body weight unchanged.
        </p>
      </details>

      <!-- FAQ: BMR vs TDEE -->
      <details class="group bg-canvas-elevated border border-hairline rounded-xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between font-semibold text-ink text-sm sm:text-base">
          <span>What is the difference between BMR and TDEE?</span>
          <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
            <svg fill="none" height="20" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"></path></svg>
          </span>
        </summary>
        <p class="text-body mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">
          BMR (Basal Metabolic Rate) is the baseline number of calories required to sustain involuntary biological life at complete rest (brain function, heart beating, respiration). TDEE (Total Daily Energy Expenditure) is your BMR plus all calories burned through daily movement, digestion, and structured exercise. TDEE is always higher than BMR, and you should avoid consuming fewer calories than your BMR for extended periods.
        </p>
      </details>

